 #uuid "a5399e66-ab2b-5a09-bda0-c08840a62517" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376578} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74385230-49f0-5ccc-8b1d-e6639ec7e848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9n-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376586} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c964d1-08cb-51c1-a3d2-d470d8558b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{n}^{2}-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376587} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf572ed-6590-542e-83da-1dc4340f3685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{n}^{2}-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376588} {:answer/id #uuid "1d48e573-951e-5595-ab9b-dcd607bc7249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{n}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c964d1-08cb-51c1-a3d2-d470d8558b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{n}^{2}-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376587} :answer-field/id #uuid "811ac29a-d33c-5a01-a379-2808de32f993" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1504bd46-29b9-5e14-ada0-f70ddc5a3734" :question/math-academy-id "q-184579" :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $ε.$\n\nFirst, simplifying $|{a}_{n}-L|,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}+\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3(1-{n}^{2})}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}+\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3-3{n}^{2}+3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε,$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f7e99e-94b4-5e9b-9bad-05d7b6014436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f7e99e-94b4-5e9b-9bad-05d7b6014436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377015} :answer-field/id #uuid "db8bfa7f-442b-51d5-808b-ac5c79ddf792" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78d75d6-55e8-599e-8254-758bdadb9493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78d75d6-55e8-599e-8254-758bdadb9493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b895470a-054b-5ad4-a5ff-8d5bb48cbf4a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af117d8d-702c-5f89-85a6-96a05f202f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af117d8d-702c-5f89-85a6-96a05f202f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377019} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ff6a4d-d728-5072-81ce-f0c70fcf798b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4b9a14d-317b-576b-b67c-05410d05c2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9a14d-317b-576b-b67c-05410d05c2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377021} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ca53cf-44db-561d-8ba9-ab83738f1d5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecbe735a-c5cb-5feb-b4b2-8a45d1e58c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecbe735a-c5cb-5feb-b4b2-8a45d1e58c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377023} :answer-field/id #uuid "8272e4c5-2429-505b-927b-2750e3b5d005" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1a8e997-11f9-5693-8fe0-4790fffa9c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-253879" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi ))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi ))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\pi ),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047fb130-8e41-5582-9c42-b83a6bb1146b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047fb130-8e41-5582-9c42-b83a6bb1146b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026} :answer-field/id #uuid "acae7b39-7953-5587-912c-6bed72a6c056" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c17988-23a4-572d-8b74-6c4722e3c968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c17988-23a4-572d-8b74-6c4722e3c968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028} :answer-field/id #uuid "5705bbf5-4328-58b5-b584-6e1e7194f2ef" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0364ea93-0af0-54d2-be0c-5dfd79fa6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0364ea93-0af0-54d2-be0c-5dfd79fa6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e48ce1-6107-5825-801a-1e0617853f5e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377029} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b8a538-70dd-5e5b-b76e-8e96ca8ca561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b8a538-70dd-5e5b-b76e-8e96ca8ca561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd78b681-ad83-5968-92bf-a31700f6b55b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a06eeb0-50da-58ee-8b6f-2f69fa647f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a06eeb0-50da-58ee-8b6f-2f69fa647f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9e2d83-4b34-5dcb-8256-50eb7278faf4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07f3343a-f24c-59e9-b03d-4ce328a45d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-253891" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $2$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
